{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"# Phase 2 COMPLETE — fix landed for review as Gerrit change 1971\n\nCommit `8a1ce463d9`, branch `fix-janitor-peccary` off `origin/main`.\nhttps://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/c/rebar/+/1971\n\n## Fix\n\n**AC-1 — the byte-total backstop now exists.** New `max_bytes` tunable: a cap on the store's\nOWN size in bytes, checked against the incrementally-maintained running total (never a hot-path\n`du`), resolved through the same owned seam as every other janitor knob —\n`REBAR_GATE_MAX_BYTES` > `[snapshot] max_bytes` > default. It defaults to **0 = off**,\nfollowing the `free_watermark_pct` / `reverify_seconds` precedent, so no existing deployment's\nbehaviour changes until an operator opts in; with the cap off the `byte_total` read is skipped\nentirely and the pass is bit-identical to before. An armed pass evicts LRU-by-`mtime` under the\nexisting grace window down to a target `RECLAIM_TARGET_MARGIN_PCT` below the cap — the mirror\nof the free-space hysteresis — so it overshoots rather than stalling on the threshold and\nre-firing every interval. A cap is a ceiling, not a purge.\n\nThe per-entry eviction loop moved out of `_gc_pass` into a new `_reclaim_loop`: a\nbehaviour-preserving extraction along the existing `_space_thresholds` / `_reverify_pass` seam,\nrequired to hold the complexity ratchet at `new=0 increased=0` with a third term added.\n`.github/complexity-baseline.json` untouched.\n\n**AC-2 — `materialize_tickets` is byte-accounted.** It now pays `add_bytes(entry_size(dest))`\non the won-race branch only; the cache-hit and lost-race paths deliberately do not (already\ncounted / the winner counts it), mirroring `cache.acquire`. The incremental total consequently\nagrees with the authoritative walk `startup_sweep` reconciles to. `cache` imports\n`repo_snapshot`, so the import is deferred in-body to avoid a cycle; accounting failure is\nswallowed so bookkeeping can never fail a materialization.\n\n**AC-4 — docs.** `docs/config.md` (`[snapshot]` block + prose), `docs/repo-snapshot-gates.md`\n(tunables row, \"Disk-cap behavior\", and a new \"Sizing the store-size cap\" section explaining\nthat the watermark asks \"is the DISK running out?\" — never true on a big or shared volume —\nwhile the cap bounds the cache itself), `docs/env-vars.md` regenerated, and\n`tests/unit/test_snapshot_docs.py` extended to pin `rebar_gate_max_bytes`.\n\n## AC-3 — RED → GREEN, with teeth\n\nFive tests in `tests/unit/test_snapshot_janitor.py`, all confirmed **RED for the right reason**\nbefore any fix existed, then **held out of the implementation subagent's working tree** (moved\naside on disk, path declared off-limits) so its green is evidence rather than tautology:\n\n| test | what it pins |\n|---|---|\n| `test_max_bytes_cap_evicts_when_over_budget` | cap is the only armed term → evicts LRU-first, stops at the target, survivors remain |\n| `test_max_bytes_cap_off_by_default_changes_nothing` | `DEFAULT_MAX_BYTES == 0`; an unset deployment evicts nothing |\n| `test_materialize_tickets_accounts_its_bytes` | accounted once, not double-counted on a hit, equal to the authoritative walk |\n| `test_cap_reclaims_a_tickets_entry_end_to_end` | the two halves compose on the entry kind that dominates the store |\n| `test_max_bytes_resolved_from_env_and_snapshot_table` | env > `[snapshot]` > default |\n\n**Mutation check.** Re-introducing each proven root cause returns the oracle to RED:\n\n* `over_budget = False` (re-omit the backstop) → 2 failed, 29 passed\n* drop the `add_bytes` call (re-omit the accounting) → 2 failed, 29 passed\n* restored → 31 passed\n\n**Test-modification discipline.** Two of my own test defects surfaced during validation (a\nwrong config-file path, and an LRU assertion over tied `mtime`s that was really an\norder-dependent flake). Both were corrected under the strict guard — fix fully reverted, test\nrevised, re-verified RED with no fix present, only then re-applied. Suite run 3× for order\nstability: 31 passed each time.\n\n**Assertion style.** Every assertion is on observable output — `GcResult.evicted`,\n`cache.byte_total`, entry existence — never a private name, an intermediate variable, or a grep\nof source text. No wall-clock upper bounds, so `scripts/check_wall_clock_asserts.py` needs no\nescape marker.\n\n## Replication of the original report\n\nThe two Phase-1 reproduction scripts encode the BUG in their assertions. Both now **fail**:\n\n    E1  evicted=3 (was 0)          -> \"ORIGINAL BUG ASSERT — must now FAIL (GC reclaimed)\"\n    E2  byte_total 0 -> 200004     -> \"ORIGINAL BUG ASSERT — must now FAIL (bytes accounted)\"\n\n## Gates\n\n`make lint` pass (ruff check + format-check, complexity ratchet `active=49 new=0 increased=0\nstale=0`, config-ownership, config-reads, DCO, criteria, skill frontmatter, zizmor, actionlint);\n`make typecheck` pass (598 files); module sizes `janitor.py` 510, `repo_snapshot.py` 724,\n`_config_resolvers.py` 484 — all under the 800 cap. Targeted suite 84 passed.\n\n## Fix-safety audit\n\n* **Restoration, not creation** — the cap is the trigger ADR 0005 D5 and this store's own\n  originating story already scoped; legitimacy was `incomplete-implementation` with that design\n  authority cited, so completing it is restoration. No new entry point, CLI command, or mutating\n  external call.\n* **No new dependency** — stdlib and existing internal modules only.\n* **No test weakened** — the single pre-existing test touched\n  (`tests/unit/test_snapshot_docs.py`) gains one assertion string; nothing removed, skipped, or\n  loosened.\n\n## Step 7 — sibling sweep\n\n**Construct axis.** The class is \"a populate path into the snapshot store that does not account\nits bytes.\" Census of every `os.rename` into the store: `repo_snapshot.py:500` (`materialize`,\naccounted by its only production callers, all five inside `cache.acquire`),\n`repo_snapshot.py:693` (`materialize_tickets` — the defect, fixed), `janitor.py:186` (`_evict`,\nthe release direction, which correctly decrements). No third producer exists; `startup_sweep`\nreconciles. Class clear.\n\n**Parity axis.** Rule stated without naming the site: *every read that hits a store entry must\nrecord the hit as recency by bumping `mtime`* (ADR 0005 D4). One **MISSING** slot found —\n`materialize_tickets`' cache-hit branch (`repo_snapshot.py:676-679`) returns without\n`touch_entry`, so a hot ticket store looks maximally LRU-cold and sorts first among eviction\nvictims. **Disposition: ticket filed**, `famous-digital-dingo` (797c-a4ea-0a64-447a), linked\n`discovered_from`. Not absorbed: it is a different rule from this ticket's two ACs, it is\nadjacent to the undispatched `smart-disloyal-naga`, and because the cap ships default-off this\nchange introduces no live exposure to it.\n\n## Residual risk & prevention\n\n* **Ticket filed** — `undamaged-epidermic-kakarikis` (58a3-0756-e470-4b40), linked\n  `discovered_from`. `start_background_janitor` has exactly one production caller, the\n  review-bot's FastAPI lifespan (`src/rebar/review_bot/app.py:134-136`). Every other host that\n  resolves an attested gate — a developer laptop, a CI runner — starts no janitor at all, so its\n  store is append-only for the life of the machine. This ticket fixes the *policy*; that one is\n  about the *cadence*. Note ADR 0005 D5's \"never invoked from populate/read\" rules out a naive\n  hot-path call, so it needs a design.\n* **Ticket filed** — `famous-digital-dingo`, above.\n* **Accepted, cross-referenced** — `SnapshotHandle._cleanup` (`repo_snapshot.py:199`) is never\n  assigned: all three construction sites (`:464`, `:487`, `:515`) omit it, so `__exit__`\n  (`:212-213`) is permanently a no-op. Verified as reported; it belongs to\n  `smart-disloyal-naga` (8386-a512-4815-4e6b), which is not dispatched. Left alone rather than\n  silently absorbed.\n* **Accepted** — the cap is default-off, so it bounds nothing until an operator sets it. That is\n  deliberate (the `free_watermark_pct` precedent; a default-on cap would change eviction\n  behaviour for every existing deployment unreviewed). The review-bot deployment can set it in\n  `infra/compose/docker-compose.yml` alongside the `free_watermark_pct` it already carries;\n  that is an operator decision, not a code change, and is left to whoever tunes that host.\n\n## Portability\n\nThe trigger is a config-resolved knob read by the in-process janitor. It is not bound to any CI\nprovider, so the blocking `project.portability` criterion is satisfied. No GitHub-Actions-only\npath was introduced."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787266881395085001,"uuid":"2b6cca8a-88fc-4980-9761-81225c5ae0eb"}